the world marks a somber milestone today  international holocaust remembrance day  in memory of the six million jews who  were killed in nazi occupied germany and  beyond and as that generation who  remembers firsthand the atrocities  dwindles over time there is a renewed  push for education and sensitivity  especially as we see a rise of  anti-semitism attacks and holocaust  denial so we want to welcome gideon  taylor he is president of the claims  conference it's a non-profit advocacy  group for survivors of the holocaust and  their descendants gideon welcome to  quick take  i grew up in new york in the 1980s and  learning about the holocaust was a big  part of history class and also a very  normal part of history class and it was  very much in the culture through  newspaper articles tv reports and movies  but  that's not necessarily the case anymore  is it can you tell us first off how much  do young americans really know about the  holocaust  less and less and as we move further and  further away from the time of the  holocaust  we've seen and surveys that we've  carried out have shown a very alarming  lack of knowledge about the holocaust  lack of awareness  and that's particularly troubling coming  at a time as there are fewer and fewer  political survivors to tell their own  stories in their own words and that's  what's driven us to  really look at all different ways that  we can make sure that the lessons of the  holocaust what happened can be  transmitted to future generations and  particularly to a younger generation  right i know a recent survey found that  half of americans could not name a  single concentration camp gideon based  on that and what you just described what  do you think is the single biggest  contributing factor to this lack of  knowledge and education about the  holocaust  i think there's a lot of reasons i mean  partly it's distance in time  partly  it's fewer survivors  and partly also  i think the way in which  a younger generation learns is very  different from  how it was 5 10 15 years ago and  that is i think is what sort of driven  us to say we need to look for new ways  to reach a generation that's  lacking in knowledge and awareness and  that's what led us to looking at social  media as a critically important tool  to communicate lessons that are  important and relevant not because of  the historical fact or just because of  the struggle fact but because of what  they say about us as human beings and  our society  right so tell us about this social media  campaign that you've launched that  applauds uh people who stand up against  hate what are some of the stories that  you tell and what kind of broader impact  do you hope it will have  so the campaign is hashtag don't be a  bystander  and it is pretty much as the hashtag  says i i think what you have stories  that come from from the holocaust  remarkable stories of people  who were not bystanders and they weren't  bystanders at a time where the risks to  themselves  of of of partaking of of taking sides of  helping a a a jew  were in some cases a penalty of death so  these were people who  gave everything or risked everything in  order to do what was right and i think  the lessons that that carry for us are  as powerful in all of the issues and  challenges we face today as they were  then and that's why we want to use these  voices the voices of holocaust survivors  while we still have holocaust survivors  with us well we still have uh  uh people whose righteous  among the nations people who took those  kind of risks to help people during the  holocaust  how what can be more important than to  take those messages this holocaust day  and to transmit it to a younger  generation through a social media  campaign  through the through the medium that's so  important for communication uh with a  wider population with a younger  population  right it's incredibly powerful but on  social media and uh in the real world  we're more divided than ever right how  do you encourage people to risk  themselves for others in an era where we  can't even get people to take a  life-saving vaccine  i i think a lot of what it is that  people will do  is because of what they see done by  figures that inspire  uh by stories that move  that move them those are the things i  think that shape a lot of our conduct  not being told what to do  or being a  instructed in a classroom to do a b or c  i think what it what leads people to  action is to seek concrete examples and  some of those examples are at home or  friends or peers and sometimes they're  events or people who were at seminal  moments of history and i think that's  why the stories of holocaust survivors  and the stories of rescuers  are so powerful because  uh that's what can influence a person  can make a person say wow  that is what a person did that's  something that i can think about what  does that mean for me it's inspirational  and in in some ways aspirational as well  gideon i want to get your thoughts on  the latest news and there seems like  there's a headline on this every single  day uh a tennessee school board has  voted to remove mouse which is a graphic  novel on the holocaust from its eighth  grade curriculum apparently there are  concerns about offensive themes and  vulgar language how do you sanitize  something like the holocaust  it's very hard to  transmit in a in an effective way  a horror that's beyond comprehension how  can we understand and take  six million jews were killed  staggering beyond our comprehension and  that's why i think over time people have  looked for ways to transmit  and make  real and and make it something that  people can understand to get your hands  around what's incomprehensible and there  were films that people said were  concerned they were concerned about  things like mouse books like mouse and  others that look for innovative ways i  think we always need to be  looking for innovative ways to transmit  a message of something that's so hard to  to educate about and we need to educate  yes in scholarship and and books and  so on but we also need to find ways that  are creative and that address and meet  people where they are because you can't  educate people where you the educator  are or us the adults are you have to  meet people where they are if you want  them to understand  what happened and what is the lesson  that can be taken from it and that i  think is critically important for us i'm  really glad you bring up the importance  of these lessons gideon because  unfortunately we have consistently seen  a raise in hate crimes anti-semitism in  the u.s  i'm curious do you think that education  in our shared history can lessen the  frequency and severity of these events  and how so  i i think you can't do it without it i  think the lessons that we see from hate  crimes um  is is is exactly a lack of understanding  of history and a lack of understanding  of other communities i mean it's those  two gaps that are  what are so often leads to this cancer  in our society of uh of of hatred and  hate speech and our earlier social media  campaign also sort of built around that  it was called it started with words  hashtag it started with words and that  was a previous campaign and the message  there was one  that the holocaust didn't happen  didn't start with death camps and  auschwitz it started with what people  said and how people said it and that's  equally a lesson that is a partner  lesson to the one we talk about don't be  a bystander it started with words is  also a lesson that we hope resonates and  we believe resonates with people think  about what you say because look what it  can lead to and that's what holocaust  survivors and videos said again and  again in that campaign  and  and i think that's something that  resonates with young people and  such such those voices and giving a  megaphone to holocaust survivors so that  public and young people will hear their  voice that's what this campaign's about  well as george santayana said those who  don't know the past are condemned to  repeat it and it's something that uh you  choose to show in different ways gideon  really appreciate your time thank you so  much for joining us gideon taylor of  course talking about the holocaust  remembrance day  you
 

